@meta
id: 28-01
title: Where your config actually lives
chapter: 28
type: lesson
skills: vimrc-structure
par: 21
minutes: 4
vscode: Ctrl-Shift-P, "Open User Settings (JSON)"
open: .vimrc

@brief
Everything chapter 27 taught you — `:set`, mappings, autocommands — lasts
until you quit. A vimrc is the same commands written down, so vim runs
them for you at startup. That is the whole idea. There is no separate
configuration language.

On Linux and macOS the file is `~/.vimrc`, and anything larger than a
single file goes in `~/.vim/`. Vim records which file it read in
`$MYVIMRC`, so `:e $MYVIMRC` opens your config from inside any session
without you having to remember the path, and `:scriptnames` lists every
file vim has sourced, in the order it sourced them, with your vimrc near
the top.

The file in front of you stands in for your real `~/.vimrc`. The dojo
grades a copy, so nothing you do in this chapter can break the config you
edit with tomorrow.

It has three settings. Add a fourth, `showcmd`, below `ruler`. It puts
the half-typed command in the bottom right corner, so when you press `2d`
and forget what comes next, the screen tells you vim is still waiting.

@keys
| `:e $MYVIMRC` | open the vimrc this session loaded |
| `:scriptnames` | every file vim has sourced, in order |
| `:echo $MYVIMRC` | print the path |
| `o` | open a line below and start typing |

@hints
The new line goes at the bottom of the file. `G` gets you there.
--
`o` opens a line below the cursor and puts you in insert mode. No need
to go to the end of the last line first.
--
`Go` then `set showcmd`, then Esc and `:wq`.

@solution
    G o set showcmd <Esc> :wq<Enter>

One line, one reason for it. That is the rhythm of this whole chapter.

Two things worth knowing about load order, because they explain most
"why is my config being ignored" questions:

- Vim reads the *first* vimrc it finds and then stops looking. On this
  machine that is `~/.vimrc`. If `~/.vim/vimrc` also exists it is not
  read, which catches people out when they move their config.
- After your vimrc, vim loads filetype plugins and indent files from
  `$VIMRUNTIME` — so a `set shiftwidth=4` in your vimrc can be quietly
  overridden a moment later by the ftplugin for the language you opened.
  That is not a bug and it is not yours to fight; chapter lesson 28-13
  shows where per-language settings actually belong.

It is also worth noticing how little there is here. Your whole editor
configuration is one file in your home directory, which is why you can
copy it to a server, or into a container, and have your own editor
waiting for you there. Keep that portability in mind as the file grows.

`:scriptnames` is how you see both of those for yourself rather than
guessing. Run it now and then in a real session; the list is shorter than
you expect.

@start .vimrc
" ~/.vimrc - one line at a time, when something annoys you.

set number
set ruler

@expected .vimrc
" ~/.vimrc - one line at a time, when something annoys you.

set number
set ruler
set showcmd
